  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 PM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nA closed upper low was noted on the 19Z water vapor imagery over MT \nmoving towards the northern plains while high pressure remained over \nthe southern MS river valley. Surface obs showed a trough of low \npressure stretching from the central high plains into the northern \nplains with a warm front lifting across the forecast area into \nsouthern NEB. \n\nFor this afternoon and tonight, CAMs still indicate some isolated \nconvection developing across north central KS and there have been \nsome weak showers pestering through the early afternoon. But \nforecast soundings have the boundary layer mixing out fairly deeply \neast of the surface trough. As a result the instability is progged \nto diminish as the boundary layer moisture scours out. So chances \nfor severe storms through the evening across north central KS appear \nto be pretty conditional on there being enough instability for a \nrobust updraft to develop. If this happens, there could be some \ndamaging wind potential with an inverted V soundings and weak \neffective shear to sustain updrafts. Overall the chances for severe \nthunderstorms appear to be low. \n\nFor Tuesday afternoon through Wednesday morning, chances for showers \nand thunderstorms will increase as a weak boundary slides south into \nsouthern KS. Short term models show a similar setup of a stalled \nboundary across southern KS while moisture continues to lift north \nof the boundary. The RAP and NAM show sustained isentropic lift into \nnortheast KS as precipital water vales increase over two inches, or \nwell above the ninetieth percentile for PWs and near a record. This \nsetup could favor the redevelopment of thunderstorms Tuesday evening \nand into Wednesday morning with torrential rainfall. The 13Z NBM has \nprobabilities of 2.5 inches or more between 50 and 75 percent across \nnortheast KS and the 12Z HREF, while a little more isolated, \nsupports this idea. If storms move across the same location multiple \ntimes, it is not inconceivable to see locations get 3 or more inches \nof rain by midday Wednesday. So have posted a flood watch for the \nareas with the better probability of receiving heavy rainfall. There \nis also a risk for severe weather Tuesday afternoon ahead of the \nfront over parts of east central KS. The forecast has temps warming \ninto the middle 90s before the boundary moves through. This could \nyield MUCAPE values around 3000 J/kg depending on how boundary layer \nmoisture holds up along the boundary. Bulk shear around 30KT could \nsupport some severe potential through the afternoon hours. \n\nModels show the mid level ridging re-amplifiing over the central \nplains to end the week. This should push precip chances back north \nof the area with highs still hanging on to the lower 90s. By this \nweekend and early next week the ECMWF and GFS show a better wave \ncoming out into the plains. Ensembles show increasing spread in the \nforecast due to differences in the amplitude and timing of the wave. \nBut the larger trend of precip chances this weekend with more fall \nlike temps for the start of next week is consistent in the \nensembles. Have not made any changes from the WPC/NBM forecast but I \nwould expect there to be some refinement of the forecast as the \ndifferences narrow.",
